S=[]
S.append({"id":"bb-pkm2-x","section":"bb","title":"An inhibitor of tumor pyruvate kinase","topic":"Biochem 2 - Enzymes","minutes":9,
"passage":[
"Pyruvate kinase (PK) catalyzes the final step of glycolysis, transferring a phosphate group from phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP) to ADP to form pyruvate and ATP. Proliferating cells, including many tumor cells, express the M2 isoform (PKM2), which is allosterically activated by fructose-1,6-bisphosphate (FBP), an intermediate produced earlier in glycolysis by phosphofructokinase-1.",
"Researchers screening small molecules identified Compound X, which reduced PKM2 activity in cell lysates. To characterize its mechanism, they purified recombinant PKM2 and measured initial reaction velocities across a range of PEP concentrations at saturating ADP. Compound X was dissolved in dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) before being added to reaction mixtures. Apparent Km and Vmax values were determined from Lineweaver–Burk plots (Table 1).",
{"table":{"caption":"Table 1 Kinetic parameters of purified PKM2","head":["Condition","Km for PEP (mM)","Vmax (µmol/min/mg)"],"rows":[["PKM2 alone","0.50","100"],["+ Compound X (10 µM)","0.50","45"],["+ FBP (50 µM)","0.10","100"],["+ FBP + Compound X","0.10","46"]]}},
"In a follow-up experiment, cultured tumor cells were treated with Compound X for 6 hours, and intracellular metabolite levels were measured by mass spectrometry."],
"questions":[
{"stem":"Based on Table 1, Compound X most likely acts as a:","choices":["competitive inhibitor that binds the PEP site.","noncompetitive inhibitor that binds a site other than the active site.","uncompetitive inhibitor that binds only the enzyme–substrate complex.","allosteric activator that stabilizes the enzyme’s active conformation."],"answer":1,"skill":"Data interpretation",
"why":["A competitive inhibitor raises Km and leaves Vmax unchanged. Table 1 shows the opposite.","Km stays at 0.50 mM while Vmax drops from 100 to 45: the signature of noncompetitive inhibition at a separate site.","Uncompetitive inhibition lowers both Km and Vmax. Km did not change.","Vmax fell, so Compound X reduces activity; it is not an activator."],
"trap":"Right concept, wrong inhibitor type","rule":"Km same + Vmax down = noncompetitive. Km up + Vmax same = competitive. Both down = uncompetitive."},
{"stem":"The effect of FBP on PKM2 is best described as:","choices":["feedback inhibition, because FBP is a downstream product of the PK reaction.","feedforward activation, because FBP is produced earlier in the same pathway.","competitive activation, because FBP binds the PEP site.","covalent modification, because FBP transfers a phosphate to PKM2."],"answer":1,"skill":"Concept application",
"why":["FBP is made upstream of PK, and it activates rather than inhibits.","FBP comes from an earlier glycolytic step and activates a later enzyme: feedforward activation.","The passage says FBP is an allosteric activator; it does not bind the substrate site.","Allosteric binding is noncovalent; nothing in the passage describes phosphate transfer to the enzyme."],
"trap":"Reversed direction (upstream vs downstream)","rule":"Place the molecule in the pathway before naming the regulation: an upstream activator is feedforward."},
{"stem":"Which conclusion about Compound X is best supported by the data in Table 1?","choices":["Compound X prevents FBP from binding PKM2.","Compound X and FBP likely affect PKM2 through separate sites.","Raising the PEP concentration would fully restore activity in the presence of Compound X.","Compound X increases PKM2’s apparent affinity for PEP."],"answer":1,"skill":"Data interpretation",
"why":["With Compound X present, FBP still lowers Km to 0.10 mM, so FBP still binds.","FBP’s effect on Km and Compound X’s effect on Vmax both persist when the two are combined, so they act independently.","Noncompetitive inhibition cannot be overcome by adding more substrate; Vmax stays low.","Compound X leaves Km unchanged, so apparent affinity is unchanged."],
"trap":"Plausible mechanism the data contradicts","rule":"Test each claim against a specific row of the table before accepting it."},
{"stem":"Compared with untreated cells, tumor cells treated with Compound X would most likely show increased intracellular levels of:","choices":["pyruvate.","lactate.","phosphoenolpyruvate.","ATP produced by substrate-level phosphorylation at the PK step."],"answer":2,"skill":"Concept application",
"why":["Pyruvate is the product of the inhibited step, so it should fall.","Lactate is made from pyruvate, further downstream, so it should fall.","PEP is the substrate of the inhibited step; it accumulates when PK slows.","ATP made at the PK step is a product of the inhibited reaction, so it decreases."],
"trap":"Downstream vs upstream of the blocked step","rule":"Inhibit a step: the substrate (upstream) builds up and the products (downstream) drop."},
{"stem":"Which additional condition would best serve as a control for the method used to add Compound X?","choices":["PKM2 with DMSO but no Compound X","Reaction mixture with Compound X but no PKM2","PKM2 with a known competitive inhibitor of pyruvate kinase","PKM2 with Compound X at twice the concentration"],"answer":0,"skill":"Research design",
"why":["Compound X was delivered in DMSO, so a solvent-only (vehicle) control shows whether DMSO alone changes activity.","This checks for a nonenzymatic reaction, not for effects of the delivery solvent.","This is a positive control for inhibition type, not for the method of adding Compound X.","This tests dose dependence; it does not isolate the solvent."],
"trap":"Useful experiment, wrong control","rule":"Match the control to the variable being questioned: method of delivery = vehicle control."}]})
S.append({"id":"cp-acetate-buffer","section":"cp","title":"Buffer composition and capacity","topic":"Gen Chem 10 - Acids and Bases","minutes":9,
"passage":[
"A student investigated how buffer composition affects resistance to pH change. Four weak acids were considered for preparing a buffer near pH 5.0 (Table 1).",
{"table":{"caption":"Table 1 Acid dissociation constants at 25 °C","head":["Acid","pKa"],"rows":[["Formic acid","3.75"],["Acetic acid","4.76"],["Dihydrogen phosphate (H₂PO₄⁻)","7.21"],["Ammonium (NH₄⁺)","9.25"]]}},
"The student prepared Buffer A by dissolving 0.10 mol acetic acid and 0.10 mol sodium acetate in water to a final volume of 1.0 L at 25 °C. Buffer B was prepared by diluting 100 mL of Buffer A to 1.0 L with water. Then 0.010 mol of HCl was added to 1.0 L of each buffer, and the pH was recorded with a calibrated pH meter.",
"In a separate trial, the student titrated 25.0 mL of 0.10 M acetic acid with 0.10 M NaOH and recorded the pH after each addition."],
"questions":[
{"stem":"Which acid in Table 1 would be the best choice for preparing a buffer at pH 5.0?","choices":["Formic acid","Acetic acid","Dihydrogen phosphate","Ammonium"],"answer":1,"skill":"Concept application",
"why":["Formic acid’s pKa (3.75) is 1.25 units from 5.0, farther than acetic acid’s.","Acetic acid’s pKa (4.76) is closest to the target pH, giving the most buffer capacity at pH 5.0.","A pKa of 7.21 is more than 2 units away from 5.0.","A pKa of 9.25 is more than 4 units away from 5.0."],
"trap":"Close but not closest","rule":"Choose the acid whose pKa is nearest the target pH (within about ±1)."},
{"stem":"What was the pH of Buffer A immediately before the HCl was added?","choices":["3.76","4.76","5.76","7.00"],"answer":1,"skill":"Concept application",
"why":["This would require [A⁻]/[HA] = 0.1, but the amounts are equal.","Equal moles of acetic acid and acetate make the log term zero, so pH = pKa = 4.76.","This would require ten times more acetate than acetic acid.","A weak acid buffer does not sit at neutral pH."],
"trap":"Right equation, wrong ratio","rule":"Equal acid and conjugate base: pH = pKa, no math needed."},
{"stem":"Approximately what pH did Buffer A reach after the HCl was added?","choices":["2.00","4.67","4.76","4.85"],"answer":1,"skill":"Concept application",
"why":["pH 2.00 is what 0.010 M HCl gives in pure water; the buffer absorbs most of the acid.","HCl converts 0.010 mol acetate to acetic acid: pH = 4.76 + log(0.090/0.110) ≈ 4.76 − 0.09 = 4.67.","Adding acid changes the ratio, so the pH cannot stay exactly at the pKa.","Adding acid lowers pH; this answer moves in the wrong direction."],
"trap":"Reversed direction","rule":"Added acid turns A⁻ into HA, so [A⁻]/[HA] drops and pH dips slightly below pKa."},
{"stem":"Compared with Buffer A, Buffer B most likely had:","choices":["the same initial pH and a smaller drop in pH after HCl was added.","the same initial pH and a larger drop in pH after HCl was added.","an initial pH one unit higher and the same drop in pH after HCl was added.","an initial pH one unit lower and a larger drop in pH after HCl was added."],"answer":1,"skill":"Concept application",
"why":["Dilution lowers buffer capacity, so the drop is larger, not smaller.","Dilution keeps the acid/base ratio (same pH) but Buffer B has only 0.010 mol acetate, all consumed by the HCl, so its pH falls much more.","Diluting both components equally does not change the ratio, so the initial pH does not shift.","The initial pH is unchanged by dilution."],
"trap":"Dilution changes pH","rule":"Diluting a buffer keeps its pH but lowers its capacity."},
{"stem":"During the titration of acetic acid with NaOH, at what volume of NaOH added would the pH equal the pKa of acetic acid?","choices":["0 mL","12.5 mL","25.0 mL","50.0 mL"],"answer":1,"skill":"Data interpretation",
"why":["Before any base is added, the solution is pure weak acid, with pH below the pKa.","Equivalence is at 25.0 mL; at half of it (12.5 mL), [HA] = [A⁻] and pH = pKa.","At equivalence all the acid is converted to acetate, so the pH is above 7.","Past equivalence, excess OH⁻ makes the solution strongly basic."],
"trap":"Equivalence vs half-equivalence","rule":"pH = pKa at half the equivalence volume."}]})
S.append({"id":"ps-observer-anagrams","section":"ps","title":"Observers, arousal and task difficulty","topic":"Behavioral Sci 8 - Social Processes, Attitudes and Behavior","minutes":9,
"passage":[
"Researchers examined how the presence of others affects performance. Undergraduate participants (N = 120) were randomly assigned to complete either an easy anagram set (common five-letter words) or a difficult set (uncommon eight-letter words). Within each difficulty level, participants were randomly assigned to work alone or while a researcher watched silently from across the room.",
"Accuracy was measured as the percentage of anagrams solved in 10 minutes. Heart rate was recorded throughout as an index of physiological arousal (Table 1).",
{"table":{"caption":"Table 1 Mean accuracy and heart rate by condition","head":["Condition","Easy set, % solved","Difficult set, % solved","Mean heart rate (bpm)"],"rows":[["Alone","78","41","74"],["Observed","89","29","86"]]}},
"The researchers concluded that the presence of an observer increased arousal and that the effect of that arousal on performance depended on task difficulty."],
"questions":[
{"stem":"The pattern of accuracy results is best explained by:","choices":["social loafing.","social facilitation.","deindividuation.","group polarization."],"answer":1,"skill":"Concept application",
"why":["Social loafing is reduced effort when individual contributions are pooled and unidentifiable; here each person is individually watched.","Being observed improved performance on the easy task and impaired it on the hard task: the classic social facilitation pattern.","Deindividuation is a loss of self-awareness in crowds, not a performance effect of being watched.","Group polarization is attitudes becoming more extreme after group discussion; no discussion occurred."],
"trap":"Look-alike social concept","rule":"Watched while working alone = social facilitation. Pooled, unidentifiable group effort = social loafing."},
{"stem":"Which of the following is an independent variable in this study?","choices":["Percentage of anagrams solved","Heart rate","Presence of an observer","Participants’ prior vocabulary knowledge"],"answer":2,"skill":"Research design",
"why":["Accuracy is measured, so it is a dependent variable.","Heart rate is measured, so it is a dependent variable.","Researchers assigned participants to be observed or not, so this is manipulated.","Vocabulary was neither manipulated nor measured; random assignment spreads it across groups."],
"trap":"Measured vs manipulated","rule":"IV = what researchers assign. DV = what they measure."},
{"stem":"The results in Table 1 provide the strongest evidence for:","choices":["a main effect of task difficulty only.","an interaction between observer presence and task difficulty.","a main effect of observer presence that is the same at both difficulty levels.","no effect of observer presence on accuracy."],"answer":1,"skill":"Data interpretation",
"why":["Difficulty does matter, but this ignores that the observer’s effect reverses across difficulty levels, the study’s key finding.","Observation raised easy-task accuracy (+11) and lowered difficult-task accuracy (−12): the effect of one variable depends on the other.","The observer’s effect points in opposite directions at the two levels.","The effects are large; they only appear to cancel if you average across difficulty."],
"trap":"True statement, not the best-supported answer","rule":"If an effect flips direction across levels of another variable, it is an interaction."},
{"stem":"Which theory best accounts for the researchers’ conclusion?","choices":["Drive theory: arousal strengthens the dominant response, which tends to be correct on easy tasks and incorrect on difficult ones.","Two-factor theory: arousal is labeled as a specific emotion based on situational cues.","Cognitive dissonance theory: people change attitudes to reduce inconsistency between beliefs and actions.","Diffusion of responsibility: individuals feel less obligated to act when others are present."],"answer":0,"skill":"Concept application",
"why":["Arousal rose with an observer and helped well-learned (easy) responses while hurting difficult ones, exactly what drive theory predicts.","Two-factor theory explains how emotions are labeled, not how arousal changes task performance.","Nothing here involves conflicting beliefs and actions.","Diffusion of responsibility explains reduced helping in groups, not individual task performance."],
"trap":"Real theory, wrong domain","rule":"Match the theory to the outcome measured: arousal + performance + presence of others = drive theory."},
{"stem":"Which feature of the study most limits the generalizability of its findings?","choices":["Participants were randomly assigned to conditions.","All participants were undergraduates.","Heart rate was recorded throughout the task.","Anagram difficulty was manipulated."],"answer":1,"skill":"Research design",
"why":["Random assignment strengthens internal validity; it is not a limitation.","A sample drawn only from undergraduates may not represent other ages or populations: an external validity concern.","Recording arousal is a measurement strength.","Manipulating difficulty is what allowed the interaction to be detected."],
"trap":"Internal vs external validity","rule":"Generalizability is about who was sampled (external validity), not how they were assigned (internal validity)."}]})
S.append({"id":"cars-restoration","section":"cars","title":"Cleaning old paintings","topic":"CARS - Humanities","minutes":9,
"passage":[
"Few debates in museum conservation are as heated as those over cleaning old paintings. When a varnish darkens over centuries, it alters every color beneath it: blues turn green, whites yellow, and shadows swallow detail. Restorers who remove such varnish argue that they are recovering what the artist actually painted. Their critics answer that the “original” is a fiction—that a painting is not a fixed object but an event that continues to happen as it ages, and that the patina of time has become part of the work.",
"Both positions contain a truth, and both overreach. The restorers are right that varnish was, in most cases, meant to be transparent; a painter who chose a particular blue did not intend it to be seen through a brown glaze. To leave grime in place out of reverence for age is to honor the accident over the intention. Yet the critics are right that cleaning is never merely subtraction. Pigments themselves change. Some glazes fade, and a thin layer that once softened a contrast may now be gone. Strip the varnish, and the restorer may reveal not the painter’s harmony but a set of colors that have aged at different rates, now exposed in a balance the artist never saw.",
"What follows, I think, is not that cleaning should stop but that its justification must change. The restorer cannot claim to return a painting to the day it left the studio; that day is unrecoverable. What the restorer can do is make a judgment about which version of the work best serves our understanding of it—and should admit that it is a judgment. Museums rarely say this. Wall labels describe restorations as “revealing” or “recovering,” verbs that imply a hidden truth simply waiting to be uncovered.",
"Some will object that admitting judgment invites arbitrariness: if no state is the true one, why not repaint freely? But the absence of a single correct answer is not the absence of better and worse answers. A historian who cannot know exactly what happened in the past still distinguishes careful interpretations from careless ones. Conservation, honestly described, is closer to that kind of interpretation than to the recovery its language promises."],
"questions":[
{"stem":"The central thesis of the passage is that:","choices":["old paintings should not be cleaned, because aging has become part of the artwork.","cleaning paintings can be justified, but only as an interpretive judgment rather than a recovery of the original.","skilled restorers can return paintings to the state the artist intended.","museum wall labels are the main cause of public misunderstanding of art."],"answer":1,"skill":"Foundations of comprehension",
"why":["This is the critics’ view; the author says cleaning should not stop.","Paragraph 3 states it directly: cleaning continues, but its justification must become an admitted judgment.","The author says the studio-day original is unrecoverable.","Wall labels are one example of the problem, and “main cause” is far beyond the passage."],
"trap":"Someone else’s view presented as the author’s","rule":"Separate the author’s voice from the positions they describe. Look for “I think” or “what follows.”"},
{"stem":"The author’s attitude toward restorers who remove darkened varnish is best described as:","choices":["dismissive.","uncritical admiration.","qualified agreement.","indifference."],"answer":2,"skill":"Reasoning within the text",
"why":["The author says the restorers are right about varnish’s intended transparency.","The author also says restorers overreach when they claim to recover the original.","The author agrees with the restorers’ practice but rejects how they justify it.","The author argues at length about how restoration should be described; that is not indifference."],
"trap":"Extreme tone","rule":"When an author says both sides “contain a truth” and “overreach,” expect a moderate, qualified attitude."},
{"stem":"Based on the passage, the author would most likely agree with which statement?","choices":["All pigments change color at the same rate over time.","Removing varnish always distorts a painting’s original color balance.","Removing varnish may expose colors in a relationship the artist never saw.","Most painters intended varnish to tint their colors."],"answer":2,"skill":"Reasoning within the text",
"why":["Paragraph 2 says colors have aged at different rates.","The passage says cleaning may reveal an unbalanced set of colors, not that it always does.","This matches paragraph 2 nearly word for word, including the hedge “may.”","Paragraph 2 says varnish was in most cases meant to be transparent."],
"trap":"Extreme qualifier","rule":"Match the passage’s hedge words: “may” in the passage rules out “always” in the answer."},
{"stem":"Suppose a museum labels a newly cleaned painting: “Conservators removed later varnish and chose to present the colors as they appear today, rather than attempting to reconstruct their original appearance.” The author would most likely view this label as:","choices":["misleading, because it implies the original is recoverable.","an example of the honest description the author recommends.","evidence that cleaning should be stopped.","proof that restoration choices are arbitrary."],"answer":1,"skill":"Reasoning beyond the text",
"why":["The label explicitly declines to claim recovery of the original.","The label presents the cleaning as a choice, exactly the admitted judgment the author asks for in paragraph 3.","The author never argues for stopping cleaning.","The author rejects the idea that admitting judgment means anything goes."],
"trap":"Ignoring the new information","rule":"For new-information questions, test the scenario against the author’s stated standard."},
{"stem":"The author mentions a historian in the final paragraph primarily to:","choices":["show that conservation is a branch of historical research.","argue that judgments can be better or worse even without a single correct answer.","suggest that restorers should consult historians before cleaning a painting.","illustrate that the past can be known exactly with enough care."],"answer":1,"skill":"Reasoning within the text",
"why":["The historian is an analogy, not a claim that conservation belongs to history.","The historian answers the arbitrariness objection: no single truth, yet careful and careless interpretations still differ.","The passage never recommends consulting historians.","The passage says the historian cannot know exactly what happened."],
"trap":"Literal reading of an analogy","rule":"Ask what job the example does in the argument, not what it is literally about."}]})
